 Transmetacarpal amputation through the metacarpals **B Wrist and Forearm** 7 Wrist disarticulation separation through the wrist joint 8 Transradial amputation through the forearm below the elbow **C Elbow and Arm** 9 Elbow disarticulation through the elbow joint 10 Transhumeral amputation through the arm above the elbow **D Shoulder** 11 Shoulder disarticulation removal of the upper limb through the shoulder joint 12 Forequarter amputation removal of the upper limb together with part of the shoulder girdle --- **2 Levels of Lower Limb Amputation** **A Foot and Ankle** 1 Toe amputation 2 Ray amputation 3 Transmetatarsal amputation 4 Lisfranc amputation through the tarsometatarsal joints 5 Chopart amputation through the transverse tarsal joint 6 Symes amputation ankle disarticulation with preservation of the heel pad **B Leg** 7 Transtibial amputation through the tibia and fibula below the knee **C Knee** 8 Knee disarticulation amputation through the knee joint **D Thigh and Hip** 9 Transfemoral amputation through the femur above the knee 10 Hip disarticulation removal of the limb through the hip joint 11 Hemipelvectomy removal of the lower limb with part of the pelvis --- **3 Indications for Amputation** • Severe traumatic injury • Peripheral vascular disease with irreversible tissue loss • Uncontrolled infection/gangrene • Malignant tumour • Severe congenital deformity • Non functional painful limb • Severe burns or crush injury • Irreversible ischemia --- **4 Principles of Selecting the Level** The level should be selected to • Remove all non viable or diseased tissue • Preserve maximum useful limb length • Preserve viable skin and soft tissue coverage • Maintain adequate blood supply • Preserve important joints whenever possible • Produce a stable painless and functional residual limb • Allow effective prosthetic fitting ///////////////////// **Traumatic Spinal Cord Injuries** **Definition** Traumatic spinal cord injury SCI is an acute injury to the spinal cord caused by an external mechanical force resulting in temporary or permanent impairment of motor sensory and autonomic functions below the level of injury --- **Causes** • Road traffic accidents • Falls from height • Sports injuries especially diving • Direct blow to the spine • Gunshot or penetrating injuries • Crush injuries • Hyperflexion or hyperextension injuries • Fracture dislocation of the vertebral column --- **Clinical Features** Clinical presentation depends mainly on the level severity and completeness of spinal cord injury **1 Motor disturbances** • Muscle weakness or paralysis below the level of injury • Paraplegia paralysis of both lower limbs • Quadriplegia/tetraplegia paralysis of all four limbs following cervical cord injury • Loss of voluntary movements • Muscle weakness may be flaccid initially because of spinal shock • Later spasticity and hyperreflexia may develop in upper motor neuron lesions --- **2 Sensory disturbances** • Loss or reduction of sensation below the level of injury • Loss of • Pain • Temperature • Touch • Pressure • Vibration • Proprioception • Paraesthesia or numbness • A clearly defined sensory level may be present --- **3 Reflex changes** **Spinal shock** Immediately after a significant acute SCI • Flaccid paralysis • Loss of deep tendon reflexes • Loss of superficial reflexes • Absent bulbocavernosus reflex may occur • Areflexia below the level of injury As spinal shock resolves reflexes may return and become exaggerated in upper motor neuron injuries --- **4 Autonomic disturbances** **Bladder dysfunction** • Urinary retention • Urinary incontinence • Neurogenic bladder **Bowel dysfunction** • Constipation • Faecal incontinence • Neurogenic bowel **Sexual dysfunction** • Erectile/ejaculatory dysfunction • Altered sexual sensation Other autonomic abnormalities may include impaired sweating and temperature regulation --- **5 Respiratory Features** Respiratory impairment depends on the level of injury • Difficulty in deep inspiration • Weak cough • Retained respiratory secretions • Reduced vital capacity • Respiratory muscle paralysis • Respiratory failure in high cervical injuries Injury involving C3 C5 can compromise diaphragmatic function because the phrenic nerve arises from these cervical segments --- **6 Cardiovascular Features** Particularly with cervical and high thoracic injuries • Hypotension • Bradycardia • Peripheral vasodilation • Neurogenic shock • Cardiac rhythm disturbances Neurogenic shock results from loss of sympathetic outflow and is particularly associated with injuries above approximately T6 --- **7 Clinical Patterns of Incomplete SCI** **A Central Cord Syndrome** • Usually follows cervical hyperextension injury • Upper limbs are affected more than lower limbs • Hand weakness may be prominent • Sensory disturbance may occur **B Brown Séquard Syndrome** Due to hemisection of the spinal cord • Ipsilateral motor weakness • Ipsilateral loss of proprioception/vibration • Contralateral loss of pain and temperature **C Anterior Cord Syndrome** • Motor paralysis below the lesion • Loss of pain and temperature • Relative preservation of dorsal column functions such as vibration and proprioception --- **8 Complete and Incomplete Injury** **Complete SCI** • No sensory or motor function is preserved in the sacral segments S4 S5 • No sacral sparing • Classified as AIS/ASIA Grade A **Incomplete SCI** • Some sensory and/or motor function is preserved below the neurological level including sacral function • Classified as AIS B D depending on the amount of preserved function • AIS E normal motor and sensory examination --- **9 Clinical Examination** Assessment should include • Level of neurological injury • Motor power • Sensory examination • Deep tendon reflexes • Anal sensation and voluntary anal contraction • Muscle tone • Respiratory function • Bladder and bowel function • Autonomic function The International Standards for Neurological Classification of Spinal Cord Injury ISNCSCI/ASIA examination is used to determine the neurological level and severity of injury --- **10 Important Complications** • Respiratory failure • Neurogenic shock • Spinal shock • Pressure sores • Deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism • Urinary tract infection • Neurogenic bladder and bowel • Contractures • Spasticity • Autonomic dysreflexia • Chronic pain • Permanent paralysis","thinking":"**Prioritizing Specific Tools**\n\nI'm focusing on the importance of selecting the most appropriate tool for each task. The goal is to maximize efficiency. I am making an active effort to ensure that I always choose the correct approach to each problem or challenge, such as choosing a relevant 'view_file' utility where possible.\n\n\n**Refining Tool Usage**\n\nI'm now implementing strict rules to optimize my tool usage, specifically avoiding `cat` for file creation/appending, preferring `grep_search` over nested `grep` commands, and generally avoiding `ls`, `cat`, `grep`, and `sed` for their basic functionalities. I'm focusing on explicit tool selection and considering alternatives before execution, particularly recalling crucial instructions regarding selecting from a list of tools. I'm starting to apply these rules to formatting a long medical text.\n\n\n**Analyzing Textual Structure**\n\nI am now focusing on parsing this medical text, carefully identifying the main sections and subsections as I move forward. I'm prioritizing the exact placement of slashes, numbers and bullet points, and the three-dash separators, as well as special characters. For block one, I have identified \"Fractures of the Upper limb\" as a main topic and am extracting the first subheading now.\n\n\n**Clarifying Topic Hierarchy**\n\nI'm now refining the hierarchy of topics based on the structure of the input text. I'm focusing on the separators, particularly the \"⸻\" (em dash or similar character) and the three-dash separators (\"---\"). It seems the em-dash-like character signals a main topic boundary