{"step_index":5,"source":"USER_EXPLICIT","type":"USER_INPUT","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-06T13:09:05Z","content":"<USER_REQUEST>\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences: High-Yield Revision\r\nComprehensive 45-Question Master Bank (02-Mark Definitions, Features & Classifications)\r\n45 EXAM QUESTIONS 2-MARK STANDARD FORMAT RESPIRATORY • CVS • ICU • DERMATOLOGY • OB-GYN\r\nPART I: RESPIRATORY PATHOLOGY & PULMONARY DISORDERS\r\n01 Bronchial Asthma\r\nDefinition:A chronic inflammatory disorder of the airways characterized by bronchial hyperresponsiveness, chronic\r\ninflammation, and recurrent episodes of reversible airflow obstruction and bronchospasm.\r\nCommon Triggers:Inhaled allergens (pollen, dust mites, animal dander), viral respiratory tract infections, cold ambient air,\r\nphysical exertion, atmospheric smoke/pollutants, and medications (aspirin, non-selective beta-blockers).\r\n02 Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)\r\nDefinition:A common, preventable, and treatable pulmonary disease characterized by persistent, usually progressive airflow\r\nlimitation caused by a combination of small airway disease (obstructive bronchiolitis) and parenchymal destruction\r\n(emphysema).\r\nMajor Risk Factors:Tobacco cigarette smoking (primary risk factor), exposure to biomass fuel smoke and poorly ventilated\r\nindoor cooking, occupational dusts and chemical fumes, ambient air pollution, and genetic α1-antitrypsin deficiency.\r\n03 Tuberculosis (TB)\r\nDefinition:A chronic, communicable granulomatous bacterial infection caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, primarily\r\naffecting the pulmonary parenchyma but capable of hematogenous or lymphatic dissemination to extrapulmonary organs.\r\nCommon Modes of Transmission:Inhalation of microscopic airborne droplet nuclei (1 to 5 μm in diameter) expelled when a\r\npatient with active pulmonary or laryngeal tuberculosis coughs, sneezes, speaks, or sings.\r\n04 Bronchiectasis\r\nDefinition:A chronic pulmonary disorder characterized by permanent, abnormal dilation and destruction of the elastic and\r\nmuscular components of the bronchial walls, driven by a vicious cycle of recurrent infection and transmural inflammation.\r\nCommon Clinical Features:Chronic persistent cough productive of copious, foul-smelling, purulent sputum; recurrent\r\nhemoptysis; progressive dyspnea; coarse inspiratory crackles and rhonchi on auscultation; and digital clubbing.\r\n05 Emphysema\r\nDefinition:An anatomically defined pulmonary disorder characterized by abnormal, permanent enlargement of the airspaces\r\ndistal to the terminal bronchioles, accompanied by destruction of alveolar walls without gross fibrosis.\r\nMajor Pathological Changes:Proteolytic breakdown of alveolar elastin fibers, loss of alveolar gas-exchanging surface area,\r\nloss of radial elastic traction causing expiratory airway collapse, pulmonary capillary bed depletion, and bullae formation.\r\n06 Pleural Effusion\r\nDefinition:An abnormal accumulation of fluid within the pleural space exceeding the physiological volume (> 15 mL), reflecting\r\nan imbalance between pleural fluid production and lymphatic absorption.\r\nCommon Causes:\r\nTransudative: Congestive heart failure, liver cirrhosis with ascites, nephrotic syndrome, severe hypoalbuminemia.\r\nExudative: Parapneumonic bacterial effusion, pulmonary tuberculosis, malignancy (bronchogenic carcinoma, metastases),\r\npulmonary embolism.\r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (2-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 45 RAPID REVISION GUIDE\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series Page 1 of 8\r\n07 Pneumothorax\r\nDefinition:The abnormal presence or accumulation of free air or gas within the pleural cavity between the parietal and visceral\r\npleura, disrupting negative intrapleural pressure and causing partial or complete ipsilateral lung collapse.\r\nMajor Types:\r\nSpontaneous: Primary (rupture of subpleural apical blebs in healthy young adults) or Secondary (complication of COPD,\r\nTB, abscess, or cystic fibrosis).\r\nTraumatic: Penetrating or blunt thoracic trauma, including iatrogenic causes (central venous catheterization, pleural tap).\r\nTension: Medical emergency where a one-way valve effect builds positive intrapleural pressure, shifts the mediastinum,\r\nand compromises venous return.\r\n08 Respiratory Failure\r\nDefinition:An acute or chronic failure of the respiratory system to maintain adequate gas exchange, defined objectively as\r\narterial hypoxemia (PaO2 < 60 mmHg) with or without hypercapnia (PaCO2 > 50 mmHg) at rest on room air at sea level.\r\nMajor Types:\r\nType 1 (Hypoxemic):PaO2 < 60 mmHg with normal or reduced PaCO2 ; caused by V/Q mismatch or shunt (e.g., ARDS,\r\nsevere pneumonia, acute pulmonary edema).\r\nType 2 (Hypercapnic):PaCO2 > 50 mmHg with concurrent hypoxemia; caused by alveolar hypoventilation or ventilatory\r\npump failure (e.g., severe COPD, Guillain-Barré, opioid overdose).\r\n09 Interstitial Lung Disease (ILD)\r\nDefinition:A heterogeneous group of non-neoplastic pulmonary disorders characterized by diffuse chronic inflammation,\r\ncellular infiltration, and progressive collagen deposition/fibrosis within the alveolar walls and perilobular interstitium.\r\nCommon Types:Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis (IPF), connective tissue disease-associated ILD (systemic sclerosis,\r\nrheumatoid arthritis), sarcoidosis, hypersensitivity pneumonitis, and occupational pneumoconioses (silicosis, asbestosis).\r\n10 Cystic Fibrosis (CF)\r\nDefinition:An autosomal recessive multisystem disorder caused by mutations in the CFTR gene on chromosome 7, leading to\r\nabnormal epithelial chloride and bicarbonate transport and secretion of viscous mucus.\r\nMajor Respiratory Manifestations:Thick endobronchial mucus obstruction; recurrent chronic bacterial infections\r\n(Pseudomonas aeruginosa, S. aureus); diffuse bronchiectasis; recurrent hemoptysis; and chronic rhinosinusitis with nasal\r\npolyposis.\r\nPART II: CARDIOVASCULAR PATHOLOGY & HEMODYNAMICS\r\n11 Atherosclerosis\r\nDefinition:A chronic, progressive inflammatory disease of large and medium-sized elastic and muscular arteries characterized\r\nby the formation of fibrofatty intimal plaques (atheromas) that compromise lumen caliber and vessel elasticity.\r\nMajor Risk Factors:\r\nModifiable: Dyslipidemia (elevated LDL, low HDL), systemic hypertension, cigarette smoking, diabetes mellitus, visceral\r\nobesity, and physical inactivity.\r\nNon-modifiable: Advancing age, male sex, and familial or genetic predisposition to premature coronary artery disease.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (2-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 45 RAPID REVISION GUIDE\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series Page 2 of 8\r\n12 Thrombosis and Embolism\r\nThrombosis:The pathological formation of a solid or semisolid mass (thrombus) composed of fibrin, aggregated platelets, and\r\ncellular elements within the unruptured vascular tree or cardiac chambers during life.\r\nEmbolism:The physical occlusion of a vascular lumen by a detached intravascular physical mass (thromboembolus, fat\r\ndroplet, air bubble, amniotic fluid) carried by the blood current away from its point of origin.\r\n13 Ischemic Heart Disease (IHD)\r\nDefinition:A spectrum of cardiac disorders resulting from an absolute or relative imbalance between myocardial oxygen\r\ndemand and coronary arterial blood supply, predominantly caused by fixed atherosclerotic narrowing.\r\nMajor Risk Factors:Hypercholesterolemia/dyslipidemia, systemic arterial hypertension, tobacco cigarette smoking, diabetes\r\nmellitus, metabolic syndrome, advancing age, and family history of early ischemic heart disease.\r\n14 Congenital Heart Disease (CHD)\r\nDefinition:Macroscopic structural or functional defects of the heart or great intrathoracic vessels that are present at birth,\r\nresulting from faulty embryonic cardiovascular morphogenesis.\r\nClassification:\r\nAcyanotic (Left-to-Right Shunt / Obstructive): Ventricular Septal Defect (VSD), Atrial Septal Defect (ASD), Patent Ductus\r\nArteriosus (PDA), Coarctation of the Aorta, Pulmonary Stenosis.\r\nCyanotic (Right-to-Left Shunt): Tetralogy of Fallot (TOF), Transposition of the Great Arteries (TGA), Tricuspid Atresia,\r\nTruncus Arteriosus, Total Anomalous Pulmonary Venous Return (TAPVR).\r\n15 Rheumatic Heart Disease (RHD)\r\nDefinition:Chronic, permanent cardiac valvular damage resulting from single or recurrent episodes of acute rheumatic fever,\r\nan autoimmune inflammatory reaction triggered by Group A β-hemolytic streptococcal pharyngitis.\r\nCommon Complications:Chronic valvular stenoses or regurgitations (predominantly mitral and aortic valves), congestive\r\ncardiac failure, atrial fibrillation with atrial thrombus formation, infective endocarditis, and cardioembolic stroke.\r\n16 Cardiac Failure (Heart Failure)\r\nDefinition:A complex clinical syndrome caused by structural or functional impairment of ventricular filling or blood ejection,\r\nrendering the heart incapable of meeting the metabolic demands of tissues at normal venous pressures.\r\nMajor Types:\r\nBy Ejection Fraction: Heart Failure with Reduced EF (HFrEF, LVEF ≤ 40%) vs. Heart Failure with Preserved EF (HFpEF, \r\nLVEF ≥ 50%).\r\nBy Ventricular Dominance: Left-sided heart failure (pulmonary congestion) vs. Right-sided heart failure (systemic venous\r\ncongestion).\r\nBy Time-Course: Acute decompensated heart failure vs. Chronic stable heart failure.\r\n17 Cardiac Arrest\r\nDefinition:The abrupt cessation of effective mechanical cardiac pump activity resulting in the immediate collapse of systemic\r\nblood flow, characterized by unresponsiveness, absent carotid pulse, and apnea or agonal breathing.\r\nImmediate Emergency Response:\r\nConfirm arrest, shout for help, and activate the emergency response system (Code Blue / AED retrieval).\r\nInitiate immediate high-quality CPR: chest compressions at 100–120/min, 5–6 cm depth, 30:2 ratio with ventilations.\r\nAttach AED/defibrillator; analyze rhythm and deliver immediate DC shock if shockable (VF or pulseless VT).\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (2-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 45 RAPID REVISION GUIDE\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series Page 3 of 8\r\n18 Cardiomyopathy\r\nDefinition:A diverse group of intrinsic myocardial disorders associated with mechanical and/or electrical cardiac dysfunction,\r\nnot secondary to coronary disease, systemic hypertension, valvular lesions, or congenital heart disease.\r\nMajor Types:Dilated Cardiomyopathy (DCM), Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (HCM), Restrictive Cardiomyopathy (RCM), and\r\nArrhythmogenic Right Ventricular Cardiomyopathy (ARVC).\r\n19 Infective Endocarditis (IE)\r\nDefinition:A serious, invasive microbial infection of the endocardial surface of the heart, characterized by the formation of\r\nbulky, friable vegetations composed of fibrin, platelets, and microorganisms on the cardiac valves.\r\nCommon Complications:Acute valvular regurgitation, congestive heart failure, perivalvular ring abscesses, systemic septic\r\nembolization (cerebral stroke, splenic/renal infarction), mycotic aneurysms, and glomerulonephritis.\r\n20 Shock\r\nDefinition:A life-threatening state of acute systemic circulatory failure characterized by inadequate tissue and cellular\r\nmicrovascular perfusion, resulting in cellular dysoxia, anaerobic metabolism, and progressive multi-organ failure.\r\nMajor Types:\r\nHypovolemic: Depletion of intravascular volume (acute hemorrhage, severe dehydration, plasma loss in burns).\r\nCardiogenic: Primary pump failure (acute myocardial infarction, severe acute myocarditis, malignant arrhythmias).\r\nDistributive: Profound systemic vasodilation (septic shock, anaphylactic shock, neurogenic spinal shock).\r\nObstructive: Physical mechanical impedance to cardiac filling or output (cardiac tamponade, tension pneumothorax,\r\npulmonary embolism).\r\nPART III: EMERGENCY CARE, CRITICAL CARE & CLINICAL DIAGNOSTICS\r\n21 Hemorrhage\r\nDefinition:The escape or extravasation of whole blood from the intravascular lumen into extravascular tissues, body cavities,\r\nor the external environment following disruption, trauma, or ulceration of vessel walls.\r\nMajor Types:\r\nBy Vessel: Arterial (pulsatile, bright red), Venous (steady, dark red), Capillary (generalized oozing).\r\nBy Location: External (visible bleeding) vs. Internal (hidden in serous cavities/tissues: hemothorax, hemoperitoneum).\r\nBy Timing: Primary (at time of trauma), Reactionary (within 24 hours), Secondary (7–14 days later due to sepsis).\r\n22 Septicemia\r\nDefinition:A severe, life-threatening systemic infection caused by the active multiplication and persistence of virulent bacteria\r\nand their toxins in the circulating blood, precipitating a dysregulated systemic host inflammatory response.\r\nCommon Clinical Features:High swinging fever with shaking chills (rigors) or hypothermia (< 36°C), resting tachycardia (> 90\r\nbpm), tachypnea (> 20 bpm), systemic arterial hypotension, altered mental status, diaphoresis, and progressive oliguria.\r\n23 Basic Life Support (BLS)\r\nDefinition:The standardized level of non-invasive emergency medical care provided to victims of cardiopulmonary arrest,\r\nacute airway obstruction, or drowning prior to the availability of advanced cardiac life support (ACLS).\r\nMain Components:Early recognition of cardiac arrest and activation of emergency response; high-quality cardiopulmonary\r\nresuscitation (CPR) with emphasis on chest compressions; early automated external defibrillation (AED); and foreign-body\r\nairway clearance maneuvers.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (2-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 45 RAPID REVISION GUIDE\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series Page 4 of 8\r\n24 Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR)\r\nDefinition:An emergency life-saving procedure consisting of external rhythmic manual chest compressions combined with\r\npositive-pressure rescue ventilations delivered to a victim in cardiac or respiratory arrest.\r\nPrimary Purpose:To maintain artificial forward perfusion to the coronary arteries and brain, sustaining minimal vital organ\r\ncellular oxygenation and viability until spontaneous circulation (ROSC) is restored or definitive therapy is delivered.\r\n25 Mechanical Ventilation\r\nDefinition:An advanced supportive medical intervention in which an automated machine (ventilator) is used to assist or\r\ncompletely replace spontaneous pulmonary gas exchange and work of breathing.\r\nTwo Major Indications:\r\nAcute respiratory failure refractory to conservative therapy (severe hypoxemic ARDS or acute hypercapnic COPD\r\ndecompensation).\r\nAirway protection and loss of neurological respiratory drive (severe coma with GCS ≤ 8, acute drug toxicity, or general\r\nanesthesia).\r\n26 Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) Analysis\r\nDefinition:A quantitative point-of-care laboratory evaluation performed on whole arterial blood (commonly sampled from the\r\nradial artery) to measure partial pressures of gases (PaO2 , PaCO2), pH, and bicarbonate concentrations (HCO3\r\n−).\r\nClinical Significance:Provides rapid objective assessment of alveolar ventilation, pulmonary oxygenation efficiency, and acid\r\nbase homeostasis (metabolic/respiratory acidosis and alkalosis), guiding ventilator management and critical care therapy.\r\n27 Electrocardiogram (ECG)\r\nDefinition:A non-invasive diagnostic recording of the bioelectrical potentials and cardiac vector voltages generated by\r\nmyocardial depolarization and repolarization, recorded through skin surface electrodes.\r\nMajor Waves:\r\nP wave: Atrial depolarization.\r\nQRS complex: Rapid ventricular depolarization (and masked atrial repolarization).\r\nT wave: Ventricular repolarization. (U wave: Inconstant, repolarization of Purkinje fibers / papillary muscles).\r\n28 Oxygen Therapy\r\nDefinition:The controlled medical administration of supplemental oxygen at concentrations higher than ambient air (FiO2 >\r\n21%) to prevent, treat, or reverse tissue hypoxia and alleviate cardiopulmonary work.\r\nCommon Indications:Documented acute hypoxemia (SpO2 < 90% or PaO2 < 60 mmHg); acute myocardial infarction; systemic\r\nshock states; severe trauma; and acute carbon monoxide poisoning.\r\nPART IV: DERMATOLOGY & INFECTIOUS DISEASES\r\n29 Psoriasis\r\nDefinition:A chronic, immune-mediated, non-contagious systemic inflammatory dermatosis driven by T-helper lymphocytes,\r\nresulting in marked hyperproliferation and abnormal differentiation of epidermal keratinocytes.\r\nCharacteristic Clinical Feature:Sharply demarcated, erythematous plaques topped with silvery-white, micaceous scales on\r\nextensor surfaces, exhibiting pinpoint capillary bleeding upon scale removal (Auspitz sign) and lesion development at sites of\r\ninjury (Koebner phenomenon).\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (2-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 45 RAPID REVISION GUIDE\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series Page 5 of 8\r\n30 Eczema (Dermatitis)\r\nDefinition:A non-contagious, inflammatory skin disorder characterized histologically by epidermal spongiosis and clinically by\r\nintense pruritus, erythema, edema, and vesiculation acutely, progressing to lichenification chronically.\r\nCommon Types:Atopic dermatitis, Allergic contact dermatitis, Irritant contact dermatitis, Seborrheic dermatitis, and Dyshidrotic\r\neczema (pompholyx).\r\n31 Urticaria (Hives)\r\nDefinition:A transient vascular cutaneous reaction characterized by the abrupt appearance of intensely pruritic, erythematous,\r\nedematous plaques with central pallor (wheals) caused by mast-cell histamine release.\r\nCommon Triggers:Food allergens (shellfish, nuts, eggs), drugs (penicillin, NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors), viral or parasitic\r\ninfections, insect envenomation, and physical triggers (dermographism, heat, cold, localized pressure).\r\n32 Acne (Acne Vulgaris)\r\nDefinition:A chronic inflammatory disease of the pilosebaceous units driven by follicular hyperkeratinization, sebum\r\noverproduction under androgenic influence, Cutibacterium acnes proliferation, and perifollicular inflammation.\r\nCommon Types:\r\nNon-inflammatory: Open comedones (blackheads) and closed comedones (whiteheads).\r\nInflammatory: Papular acne, pustular acne, and nodulocystic acne (including severe forms like acne conglobata).\r\n33 Vitiligo\r\nDefinition:An acquired, progressive chronic autoimmune skin disease characterized by the targeted immune-mediated\r\ndestruction of epidermal melanocytes, leading to complete absence of melanin pigment.\r\nCharacteristic Clinical Presentation:Asymptomatic, well-circumscribed, chalk-white or amelanotic macules and patches of\r\nnormal cutaneous texture, frequently exhibiting bilateral symmetrical distribution over extensor surfaces, hands, and periorificial\r\nareas.\r\n34 Leprosy (Hansen's Disease)\r\nDefinition:A chronic granulomatous infection caused by Mycobacterium leprae, an obligate intracellular acid-fast bacillus\r\ndisplaying unique tropism for the skin, upper respiratory mucosa, and peripheral nerve Schwann cells.\r\nMajor Neurological Complications:Peripheral sensorimotor neuropathy with painless glove-and-stocking sensory loss, nerve\r\nthickening, neuropathic plantar ulcerations, and peripheral paralyses: claw hand (ulnar nerve), wrist drop (radial nerve), foot\r\ndrop (common peroneal nerve), and lagophthalmos (facial nerve).\r\nPART V: CLINICAL NUTRITION, MATERNAL HEALTH & OB-GYN\r\n35 Protein-Energy Malnutrition (PEM)\r\nDefinition:A broad spectrum of nutritional deficiency disorders resulting from insufficient dietary intake, poor absorption, or\r\nhypercatabolic wasting of proteins and energy needed to sustain growth and physiological homeostasis.\r\nMajor Types:\r\nKwashiorkor: Predominant protein deficiency with adequate carbohydrates; presents with generalized pitting edema,\r\n\"flaky-paint\" dermatosis, sparse depigmented hair, moon facies, and hepatomegaly.\r\nMarasmus: Severe deficiency of both total calories and protein; presents with extreme somatic muscle wasting, loss of\r\nsubcutaneous fat, and wizened \"old-man\" facies without edema.\r\nMarasmic Kwashiorkor: Severe muscle and fat wasting combined with peripheral edema.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (2-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 45 RAPID REVISION GUIDE\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series Page 6 of 8\r\n36 Vitamin Deficiency Disease\r\nDefinition:A specific pathologic nutritional disorder caused by chronic inadequate dietary intake, impaired gastrointestinal\r\nabsorption, or metabolic derangement of one or more vital micronutrients (vitamins).\r\nTwo Examples:\r\nScurvy: Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) deficiency; impaired collagen synthesis resulting in swollen bleeding gums, perifollicular\r\npetechiae, subperiosteal hematomas, and delayed wound healing.\r\nRickets (in children) / Osteomalacia (in adults): Vitamin D deficiency; impaired mineralization of bone matrix and growth\r\nplates, leading to bowing of long legs and rachitic rosary.\r\n37 Antenatal Care (ANC)\r\nDefinition:Comprehensive, continuous clinical, nutritional, and preventive healthcare provided by skilled medical professionals\r\nto a pregnant woman throughout the gestational period from conception until labor.\r\nCore Objectives:Monitor maternal-fetal physical well-being; detect and manage high-risk obstetrical complications early (pre\r\neclampsia, gestational diabetes, severe anemia); provide micronutrient prophylaxis (iron-folate); and prepare the mother for\r\nsafe delivery and breastfeeding.\r\n38 Postnatal Care (PNC)\r\nDefinition:The specialized medical, diagnostic, and supportive health surveillance provided to the mother and newborn infant\r\nimmediately after parturition through the puerperium (traditionally 6 weeks or 42 days postpartum).\r\nClinical Importance:Immediate detection and prevention of major postpartum morbidities (postpartum hemorrhage, puerperal\r\nsepsis, thromboembolism, postpartum depression), neonatal evaluation, reinforcement of exclusive breastfeeding, and\r\npostpartum contraception counseling.\r\n39 Menopause\r\nDefinition:The permanent, physiological cessation of menses resulting from the irreversible exhaustion of ovarian follicular\r\nreserves and loss of ovarian estradiol production, established clinically after 12 consecutive months of amenorrhea.\r\nCommon Symptoms:Vasomotor symptoms (hot flashes, night sweats), sleep disturbances, urogenital atrophy (vaginal\r\ndryness, dyspareunia, urinary frequency), mood swings and irritability, and decreased libido.\r\n40 Infertility\r\nDefinition:A disease of the male or female reproductive tract defined by the failure to achieve a successful clinical pregnancy\r\nafter 12 continuous months or more of regular, unprotected heterosexual intercourse without contraception.\r\nMajor Causes:\r\nFemale Factors (40–50%): Ovulatory dysfunction (PCOS), tubal occlusion/pelvic adhesions (PID), endometriosis, uterine\r\nfibroids.\r\nMale Factors (30–40%): Semen abnormalities (oligo/astheno/teratozoospermia), varicocele, endocrine disorders,\r\nobstructive azoospermia.\r\nCombined / Unexplained Factors (10–20%).\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (2-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 45 RAPID REVISION GUIDE\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series Page 7 of 8\r\n41 Urinary Incontinence\r\nDefinition:The involuntary, uncontrollable loss or leakage of urine that is objectively demonstrable and causes physical\r\nhygiene, emotional, or social impairment.\r\nMajor Types:\r\nStress Incontinence: Involuntary leakage during coughing, laughing, or lifting due to pelvic floor laxity or urethral\r\nhypermobility.\r\nUrge Incontinence: Involuntary leakage preceded by an urgent, sudden urge to void, caused by detrusor overactivity.\r\nOverflow Incontinence: Involuntary dribbling from a chronically overdistended bladder (obstruction or underactive\r\nbladder).\r\nFunctional Incontinence: Incontinence secondary to physical, cognitive, or environmental impediments preventing normal\r\ntoilet access.\r\n42 Hysterectomy\r\nDefinition:The surgical excision and removal of the uterus, categorized as total (body and cervix removed) or subtotal/\r\nsupracervical (body removed, cervix preserved), via abdominal, vaginal, or laparoscopic routes.\r\nCommon Indications:Symptomatic uterine leiomyomas (fibroids) unresponsive to medical therapy, refractory abnormal\r\nuterine bleeding, gynecological malignancies (endometrial, cervical, or ovarian cancer), severe uterine prolapse, and\r\ndebilitating endometriosis/adenomyosis.\r\n43 Dilatation and Curettage (D&C)\r\nDefinition:A minor gynecological operative procedure involving the gradual mechanical enlargement/dilatation of the cervical\r\ncanal followed by the scraping or vacuum evacuation (curettage) of the endometrial lining.\r\nKey Indications:\r\nDiagnostic: Evaluation of postmenopausal bleeding, abnormal uterine bleeding, or suspected endometrial hyperplasia and\r\nneoplasia.\r\nTherapeutic: Evacuation of retained products of conception following incomplete or missed abortion, management of acute\r\nheavy uterine bleeding, or polyp excision.\r\n44 Pre-eclampsia\r\nDefinition:A multisystem disorder of pregnancy characterized by the new development of arterial hypertension (≥ 140/90\r\nmmHg) presenting after 20 weeks of gestation in a previously normotensive female, accompanied by proteinuria and/or\r\nmaternal organ dysfunction.\r\nMajor Clinical Features:Persistent gestational hypertension, significant proteinuria (≥ 300 mg/24 h), rapid pathological facial\r\nand non-dependent hand edema, throbbing frontal headache, scotomata and visual blurriness, epigastric or right upper\r\nquadrant abdominal tenderness, and thrombocytopenia.\r\n45 Sexually Transmitted Diseases (STDs)\r\nDefinition:A heterogeneous group of communicable infectious syndromes caused by bacteria, viruses, parasites, or fungi that\r\nare predominantly spread from person to person through unprotected sexual intercourse (vaginal, anal, or oral).\r\nTwo Classical Examples:\r\nSyphilis: A chronic systemic venereal infection caused by the spirochete Treponema pallidum, presenting primarily with an\r\nindurated, painless chancre.\r\nGonorrhea: An acute purulent mucosal infection of the genitourinary tract caused by the Gram-negative diplococcus \r\nNeisseria gonorr